The liveness stack still misses a stalled stream when the latest stream ID remains unchanged, and it can also alert worker-dead without parent-process evidence. These issues block the requested liveness verification.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Treat unchanged stream IDs as growth — /home/wali/Desktop/AI BOT REBUILD/v2/backend/app/domain/liveness_stream_growth/growth_calculator.py:42-44
  When a stream already has a latest Redis ID and then stalls, the collector records repeated observations with the same `stream_id`; this function returns `1` for that window, and `evaluate_liveness()` only raises the zero-growth alert when `prediction_stream_id_growth == 0`. As a result, a process-alive trainer with no new prediction stream IDs can avoid the required zero-stream-growth alert after the first observed ID.

- [P2] Gate worker-dead alerts on parent process evidence — /home/wali/Desktop/AI BOT REBUILD/v2/backend/app/domain/trainer_liveness/evaluator.py:57-59
  When the trainer parent is absent or never observed (`trainer_pid=None`, `trainer_rss_bytes=None`) and the valid snapshot has `prediction_worker_alive=False`, this branch still emits `prediction_worker_dead`. The required failure class is process-alive/worker-dead, so this can produce critical internal-liveness alerts for cold-start or offline trainer states instead of only when parent process evidence exists.
